<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akhsitan I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akhsitan I was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a>, ruler of Shirvan in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, in the second half of the twelfth century, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuchihr-iii">Manuchihr III</a>. His court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> was a centre of Persian poetry, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaqani">Khaqani</a> served, and in 1188 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a> composed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun">Layla and Majnun</a> at his request. He fought invaders from the north with the help of the Georgian kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akhsitan-i.jpg" alt="Akhsitan I" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: American Numismatic Society · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Shirvanshah_Akhsitan_I,_minted_at_Shamakhi_between_1175_and_1194.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
